\description{
Groups the categories of a feature so that the grouping and the number of
groups maximize a Bayesian score of a constant event rate per group. A
category keeps a group of its own only when the data support it. Reports
posterior-mean WoE with credible intervals.
}
\details{
\strong{Model.} The event rates of the groups are independent
\eqn{\mathrm{Beta}(a, b)} with mean equal to the overall event rate
\eqn{\pi_0} and \eqn{\min(a, b)} equal to \code{prior_strength}. For a
group with \eqn{n_i} rows and \eqn{e_i} events, write \eqn{A_i = a + e_i}
and \eqn{B_i = b + n_i - e_i}.

\strong{Criterion.} A grouping is scored by \eqn{\sum_i f(n_i, e_i)} minus
\code{bin_penalty} for each group beyond the first. With
\code{criterion = "predictive"}, \eqn{f} is the leave-one-out log
predictive probability of the rows of the group,
\deqn{f(n_i, e_i) = e_i \log\frac{A_i - 1}{A_i + B_i - 1} +
  (n_i - e_i) \log\frac{B_i - 1}{A_i + B_i - 1} ,}
and with \code{criterion = "evidence"} it is the log marginal likelihood
\deqn{f(n_i, e_i) = \log \frac{B(A_i, B_i)}{B(a, b)} .}
Both scores fall when a group is split into groups with equal observed
event rates: by about one unit for the predictive score, and by an amount
that grows as \eqn{\frac{1}{2}\log n_i} for the evidence, which therefore
returns fewer groups in large samples. The grouping is chosen among many
candidates, so with \code{bin_penalty = 0} a feature unrelated to the
target can still be split. A positive \code{bin_penalty} controls this:
it multiplies the prior weight of a grouping by
\eqn{e^{-\mathrm{penalty}}} for each additional group.

\strong{Search.} Categories are ordered by their posterior-mean event rate
\eqn{(a + e)/(a + b + n)}, which shrinks rare categories toward
\eqn{\pi_0} before they are ordered. Ties are broken by count and then by
name, so the result does not depend on the row order. The engine then
finds, by dynamic programming, the grouping into bins contiguous in that
order that maximizes the score, subject to \code{bin_cutoff}, the limits
on the number of bins and strictly increasing posterior WoE. The search is
exact among groupings contiguous in that order; groupings that are not
contiguous are not examined.

\strong{Rare categories.} Merging two groups changes the score by
\eqn{f(n_g + n_h, e_g + e_h) - f(n_g, e_g) - f(n_h, e_h)}. A category
with few rows rarely carries enough information for a group of its own, so
with \code{bin_cutoff = 0} it is merged by the score, not by a frequency
threshold.

\strong{WoE.} As in \code{\link{ob_numerical_orbe}},
\deqn{\mathrm{WoE}_i = \psi(A_i) - \psi(B_i) - \log\frac{\pi_0}{1 - \pi_0},}
with posterior variance \eqn{\psi'(A_i) + \psi'(B_i)}. The credible
intervals are conditional on the selected bins.
}
\examples{
set.seed(123)
levels <- c(paste0("region_", 1:8), "rare_a", "rare_b")
prob <- c(rep(0.1, 8), 0.01, 0.01)
feature <- sample(levels, 3000, replace = TRUE, prob = prob)
rate <- c(0.05, 0.06, 0.10, 0.11, 0.20, 0.21, 0.35, 0.36, 0.15, 0.15)
target <- rbinom(3000, 1, rate[match(feature, levels)])

# bin_cutoff = 0 leaves the fate of the rare categories to the score
fit <- ob_categorical_orbe(feature, target, bin_cutoff = 0)

data.frame(
  bin = fit$bin, count = fit$count,
  woe = round(fit$woe, 3),
  lower = round(fit$woe_lower, 3),
  upper = round(fit$woe_upper, 3)
)

}
